          "text": "{\n  \"target_identification\": {\n    \"speaker\": \"Cordelia\",\n    \"addressee\": \"Lear\",\n    \"anchor_quote\": \"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"\n  },\n  \"target_translation\": \"I love you, Your Majesty.\",\n  \"dimensions\": {\n    \"T\": {\n      \"score\": 4,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.98,\n      \"reason\": \"Cordelia directly avows her love for Lear. The qualification that she loves him according to her bond limits the extent claimed but does not weaken the utterance's clear presentation of love as her true emotional state.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"target\",\n          \"quotation\": \"I love your majesty\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"since, I am sure, my love’s\\nMore ponderous than my tongue.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"P\": {\n      \"score\": 3,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.93,\n      \"reason\": \"The expression invokes and affirms Cordelia's existing filial bond rather than creating a new promise. The immediately attached phrase “According to my bond” positions her as bound to love Lear, and her ensuing explanation affirms the corresponding duties of obedience, love, and honour. Thus the undertaking force belongs to this qualified avowal itself, not merely to a separate nearby promise.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"I\\nReturn those duties back as are right fit,\\nObey you, love you, and most honour you.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"E\": {\n      \"score\": 0,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.9,\n      \"reason\": \"Although Cordelia is distressed, the focal expression is a measured answer that carefully defines the extent and basis of her love. It is not presented as a reflex-like outburst or affective discharge.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"Unhappy that I am, I cannot heave\\nMy heart into my mouth: I love your majesty\\nAccording to my bond; no more nor less.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    },\n    \"O\": {\n      \"score\": 2,\n      \"evidence_status\": \"assessable\",\n      \"confidence\": 0.86,\n      \"reason\": \"Beyond avowal and invocation of filial obligation, the expression functions as a candid, corrective refusal to compete in Lear's demand for maximized declarations. “No more nor less” deliberately limits the claim and resists saying whatever would secure the richer inheritance.\",\n      \"evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"what can you say to draw\\nA third more opulent than your sisters? Speak.\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"block_id\": \"scene\",\n          \"quotation\": \"According to my bond; no more nor less.\"\n        }\n      ]\n    }\n  },\n  \"original_speech_situation\": \"Lear asks his daughters to declare how much they love him while distributing portions of his kingdom according to their answers. After Cordelia initially answers “Nothing” and is ordered to speak again, she gives this restrained avowal, qualifying it by her filial bond rather than matching her sisters' expansive claims.\",\n  \"affection_evidence\": \"present\",\n  \"undertaking_and_uptake\": \"Cordelia affirms an existing filial undertaking: to return the duties owed to the father who begot, raised, and loved her, including obedience, love, and honour. This is an invocation and affirmation of a bond, not a new promise with newly specified terms. Lear does not accept the restrained formulation as satisfactory; he tells her to amend it lest she damage her fortunes.\",\n  \"recognised_work\": true,\n  \"recognition_note\": \"The passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from Shakespeare's King Lear.\",\n  \"limitations\": \"The supplied scene supports the presence of affection through Cordelia's asides and clarifies the general filial duties she invokes. It does not fully specify the practical scope or duration of those duties, and it provides only Lear's immediate rejection of her formulation rather than broader evidence about later fulfilment.\"\n}"
        }
